WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.410
Aerial observation is structured, purpose-driven visual collection from above.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.679
It can use drones, aircraft, towers, elevated cameras, or overhead imagery,

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:13.989
but every method requires context, authority, and careful interpretation.

00:00:13.989 --> 00:00:19.736
Begin with a question, bounded area, required detail, time window, environmental constraints,

00:00:19.736 --> 00:00:21.173
and explicit exclusions.

00:00:21.173 --> 00:00:26.441
Plan rules, airspace, property access, weather, lighting, obstacles, route, battery margins,

00:00:26.441 --> 00:00:28.357
recovery, and emergency options.

00:00:28.357 --> 00:00:33.625
Collect stable, well-framed imagery with an angle, field of view, overlap,

00:00:33.625 --> 00:00:36.498
and format suited to the objective.

00:00:36.498 --> 00:00:41.767
Describe visible features before interpreting them, and never infer identity, intent,

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:45.119
ownership, cause, or severity without supporting evidence.

00:00:45.119 --> 00:00:50.387
Time-based comparison depends on repeatable collection and preserved date, location, sensor,

00:00:50.387 --> 00:00:51.824
and condition context.

00:00:51.824 --> 00:00:56.613
File naming, metadata, logs, source notes, controlled originals, access restrictions,

00:00:56.613 --> 00:00:59.008
and retention make findings reviewable.

00:00:59.008 --> 00:01:02.839
The goal is useful situational awareness and evidence,

00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:05.713
not uncontrolled surveillance or instant certainty.

00:01:05.713 --> 00:01:10.023
Strong practice combines safety, image quality, documentation, privacy, proportionality,

00:01:10.023 --> 00:01:11.460
and responsible reporting.
